1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,760
This is Doing and
Feeling, the long version.

2
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:05,460
Episode eleven: Theories on Trial.

3
00:00:05,660 --> 00:00:11,210
In 1998, the neuroscientist Christof Koch
made a bet with the philosopher David

4
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:16,730
Chalmers. Koch wagered a case of fine wine
that within twenty-five years, science

5
00:00:16,780 --> 00:00:20,460
would clearly identify the brain
mechanism behind consciousness.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,890
Chalmers bet against. Twenty-five years
later, in June 2023, at the annual meeting

7
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:31,830
of the Association for the Scientific
Study of Consciousness in New York, Koch

8
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:35,610
conceded on stage and handed
over a case of Madeira.

9
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:39,940
Then the two of them renewed the
bet, with a new deadline of 2048.

10
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:44,710
The concession came at the same session
where the first results were presented from

11
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:48,670
an experiment pitting two leading theories
against each other, with their own

12
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:51,820
champions agreeing in advance
on what would count as failure.

13
00:00:52,380 --> 00:00:53,880
We'll end with that experiment.

14
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:58,180
First, the theories, and a problem
with how they've been tested.

15
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:01,660
What should a theory of
consciousness explain?

16
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:04,870
Why some contents are
conscious and others aren't.

17
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:07,600
Why we're conscious at
some times and not others.

18
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:11,060
Why experiences have the
specific character they do.

19
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:13,140
Why consciousness is unified.

20
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:17,220
And what it adds to an organism
that processes information anyway.

21
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:19,610
No theory explains all five.

22
00:01:19,660 --> 00:01:25,950
A 2022 review by Anil Seth and Tim Bayne
listed twenty-two theories in circulation

23
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,680
and examined four families in depth.

24
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:33,920
Seth has also argued for a strategy
he calls addressing the real problem.

25
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:39,310
Rather than solving or dissolving the hard
problem head-on, explain, predict, and

26
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:44,050
control the properties of experience, the
way biology dissolved the mystery of life,

27
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:49,670
not by finding a vital spark but by
explaining metabolism, reproduction, and

28
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:54,730
heredity one at a time. Whether consciousness
will yield the same way is exactly

29
00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:57,360
what the hard problem of
episode seven disputes.

30
00:01:57,640 --> 00:02:00,140
But the strategy has made
the field productive.

31
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:02,420
Where do researchers themselves stand?

32
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:06,990
A survey of one hundred and sixty-six
attendees of the field's main conference in

33
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:11,440
2018 and 2019, published
in 2022, gives a snapshot.

34
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:16,620
Predictive processing was rated promising
by about sixty-one percent of respondents,

35
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:21,910
global workspace theory by fifty-eight,
higher-order theories by fifty-four,

36
00:02:21,960 --> 00:02:26,070
integrated information theory by
forty-five, and recurrent processing by

37
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:29,820
thirty-nine. Quantum theories drew
the most votes as not promising.

38
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:34,580
About two-thirds said machines could
be conscious, now or in the future.

39
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:39,370
It was a self-selected sample, but it
tells you the field has favorites, and no

40
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:44,400
consensus. Before the theories, a
problem with the evidence for them.

41
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:51,410
In 2022, Itay Yaron, Lucia Melloni, Michael
Pitts, and Liad Mudrik built a database

42
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:55,770
of four hundred and twelve experiments
that interpreted their results in light of

43
00:02:55,820 --> 00:02:58,040
one or more of the four leading theories.

44
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:02,610
They found that whether a study ended
up supporting a given theory could be

45
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:07,040
predicted from its methodological choices
alone, before you looked at the results.

46
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:12,450
In other words, the kind of experiment a
lab chose strongly predicted which theory

47
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:13,760
it would find support for.

48
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:18,950
They also found that most studies interpreted
their findings after the fact, rather

49
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,060
than testing a theory's
critical predictions in advance.

50
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:26,850
So the field has been running the garden
of forking paths at the level of whole

51
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:31,400
paradigms. Each camp picks the
experiments its theory is good at.

52
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:36,330
That's the uncharitable version, and there's
truth in it, though nobody needs to be

53
00:03:36,380 --> 00:03:41,530
acting in bad faith. Theories grow up
around the phenomena that inspired them, and

54
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:46,440
labs specialize. But piles of confirming
studies tell you less than they seem to,

55
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:50,540
which is the reason for the field's
most important development this decade:

56
00:03:50,980 --> 00:03:52,420
adversarial collaborations.

57
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:56,680
The Templeton World Charity Foundation
has funded a series of them.

58
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:01,630
Proponents of rival theories agree in
advance on experiments whose outcomes would

59
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:05,200
embarrass at least one of them, and
neutral teams run the experiments.

60
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:10,030
The program has pitted integrated
information theory against global neuronal

61
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:14,640
workspace theory, twice; first-order
theories against higher-order ones;

62
00:04:14,860 --> 00:04:18,240
and integrated information theory
against predictive processing.

63
00:04:18,540 --> 00:04:23,350
One workshop tried to design a test pitting
integrated information theory against

64
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:28,770
Orch O.R., the quantum theory from episode
nine, and concluded that it could not

65
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:33,680
find an experiment suitable for a head-to-head
test, which is informative in itself.

66
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,580
Keep that in mind as we
go through the theories:

67
00:04:36,840 --> 00:04:41,790
what each claims, its best evidence, its
best counter-evidence, and what it implies

68
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:45,900
for machines. First,
global workspace theory.

69
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:49,920
Bernard Baars proposed it in
1988 with a theater metaphor.

70
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:54,273
Most of what the brain does happens
backstage, in many specialized processors

71
00:04:54,323 --> 00:04:57,288
working in parallel and out of awareness.

72
00:04:57,338 --> 00:04:59,872
Consciousness is the spotlight on stage:

73
00:04:59,922 --> 00:05:05,040
the content that wins a competition for
a limited-capacity workspace and gets

74
00:05:05,090 --> 00:05:09,777
broadcast to the whole audience, available
to memory, planning, language, and

75
00:05:09,827 --> 00:05:14,084
report. Stanislas Dehaene, Michel Kerszberg,
and Jean-Pierre Changeux turned this

76
00:05:14,134 --> 00:05:17,530
into a neural theory, the
global neuronal workspace.

77
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:22,698
Its hardware is a network of neurons with
long-range connections, especially in

78
00:05:22,748 --> 00:05:24,720
prefrontal and parietal cortex.

79
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:27,180
Its signature event is ignition.

80
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:31,280
When a stimulus crosses into
awareness, activity doesn't just grow;

81
00:05:31,480 --> 00:05:36,470
it suddenly amplifies and spreads across
the network, all or nothing, typically a

82
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:38,470
few hundred milliseconds
after the stimulus.

83
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:40,880
Dehaene describes four markers:

84
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:46,320
a late brainwave called the P three b, a
sudden nonlinear amplification of activity,

85
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:51,160
bursts of high-frequency oscillation, and
synchronization between distant regions.

86
00:05:51,460 --> 00:05:57,070
In 2018, Bram van Vugt, Pieter Roelfsema,
and colleagues recorded from monkeys

87
00:05:57,120 --> 00:06:01,830
detecting faint stimuli. When the monkey
reported seeing the stimulus, activity

88
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:06,620
propagated up to prefrontal cortex and
sustained itself there, all or none.

89
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:10,000
When it missed, the signal
was lost along the way.

90
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:14,640
And higher baseline activity in
prefrontal cortex predicted false alarms.

91
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:19,890
The theory explains the limited capacity
of awareness, and why consciously seen

92
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:25,170
information is available for flexible,
multi-step reasoning while unseen information

93
00:06:25,220 --> 00:06:30,490
isn't. It's also the theory most naturally
built into machines, because broadcast

94
00:06:30,540 --> 00:06:35,180
through a shared, limited-capacity medium
is something an engineer can design.

95
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:39,040
The trouble comes from the
report confound of last episode.

96
00:06:39,300 --> 00:06:44,583
In inattentional blindness experiments by
Michael Pitts and colleagues, the P three

97
00:06:44,633 --> 00:06:49,490
b appeared when stimuli were relevant to
the task, but largely vanished when people

98
00:06:49,540 --> 00:06:52,520
saw the same stimuli without
needing to report them.

99
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:56,470
An earlier signal over the back of the
brain, at around two to three hundred

100
00:06:56,520 --> 00:07:00,950
milliseconds, called the visual awareness
negativity, tracked awareness more

101
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:05,430
faithfully. That suggests the classic
marker reflects what you do with an

102
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:08,280
experience, rather than
the experience itself.

103
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:13,750
Workspace theorists reply that ignition
needn't involve report, and the argument

104
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:17,660
continues. Second, higher-order theories.

105
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:23,050
On these views, a mental state is conscious
when you are, in a suitable way, aware

106
00:07:23,100 --> 00:07:27,670
of being in it. The philosopher David
Rosenthal developed the higher-order thought

107
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:32,270
version: a perception becomes conscious
when it's accompanied by a thought that you

108
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:36,380
are having it. The classic objection
concerns empty higher-order states.

109
00:07:36,860 --> 00:07:40,590
Suppose the higher-order thought says
you're seeing red, but there's no red

110
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:41,980
perception underneath at all.

111
00:07:42,140 --> 00:07:43,520
What is it like to be you?

112
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:45,320
Rosenthal bites the bullet:

113
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:48,340
you'd seem to see red, so
you'd be conscious of red.

114
00:07:48,680 --> 00:07:53,270
Critics like Ned Block reply that the
theory has now located experience in the

115
00:07:53,320 --> 00:07:55,720
higher-order state, leaving
the perception idle.

116
00:07:56,060 --> 00:08:00,770
The most influential current version is
Hakwan Lau's perceptual reality monitoring

117
00:08:00,820 --> 00:08:06,430
theory. Sensory systems produce activity
all the time, from real inputs, from

118
00:08:06,480 --> 00:08:09,000
imagination, from memory, from noise.

119
00:08:09,480 --> 00:08:14,870
Consciousness, on Lau's view, arises when
a higher-order mechanism tags a sensory

120
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:19,060
representation as a reliable
reflection of the world, right now.

121
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:24,590
Lau has compared this to a generative
adversarial network, in which a discriminator

122
00:08:24,640 --> 00:08:27,460
learns to tell real data
from generated data.

123
00:08:27,700 --> 00:08:30,880
That gives you a mechanism
for hallucination for free.

124
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:35,300
The discriminator misfires
and tags imagination as real.

125
00:08:35,620 --> 00:08:37,930
And Lau uses it exactly that way:

126
00:08:37,980 --> 00:08:43,350
hallucinations as failures of reality
monitoring, and blindsight as the opposite

127
00:08:43,400 --> 00:08:46,260
failure, where real
signals don't get tagged.

128
00:08:46,700 --> 00:08:51,310
The evidence for this family includes
experiments in which people perform equally

129
00:08:51,360 --> 00:08:56,250
well on a task but differ in how aware
or confident they feel, with matching

130
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:58,180
differences in prefrontal activity.

131
00:08:58,440 --> 00:09:00,920
There's a cautionary
tale from this camp, too.

132
00:09:01,300 --> 00:09:06,330
A 2010 study found that magnetic stimulation
of prefrontal cortex reduced people's

133
00:09:06,380 --> 00:09:10,790
ability to judge their own accuracy without
impairing the task itself, and it was

134
00:09:10,840 --> 00:09:16,550
widely cited as support. In 2017, Daniel
Bor and colleagues tried to reproduce it

135
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:20,620
with ninety participants, then ran
a follow-up, and found no effect;

136
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:22,500
the statistics favored the null.

137
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:24,580
One more classic to downgrade.

138
00:09:24,780 --> 00:09:29,750
The main empirical objection to higher-order
theories is that large prefrontal

139
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:32,260
lesions don't seem to
abolish consciousness.

140
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:38,540
In 2017, the Journal of Neuroscience
published a paired debate on precisely this.

141
00:09:38,780 --> 00:09:44,350
One team, including Melanie Boly, Koch,
and Giulio Tononi, argued that the neural

142
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:47,360
correlates of consciousness lie
mainly in the back of the brain.

143
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:53,070
The other, Brian Odegaard, Robert Knight,
and Lau, argued that prefrontal cortex is

144
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:56,560
essential, and that the negative
findings were weaker than claimed.

145
00:09:56,940 --> 00:10:00,620
That front-versus-back fight runs
through the rest of this episode.

146
00:10:02,260 --> 00:10:05,270
Third, recurrent processing
theory, from Victor Lamme.

147
00:10:05,320 --> 00:10:10,470
When a visual stimulus arrives, a fast
feedforward sweep carries it up the visual

148
00:10:10,520 --> 00:10:15,770
hierarchy in about a tenth of a second,
enough to drive quick responses, but on this

149
00:10:15,820 --> 00:10:20,690
view unconscious. Then feedback connections
kick in, creating local recurrent

150
00:10:20,740 --> 00:10:22,510
processing within sensory cortex.

151
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:27,660
Lamme argues that this local recurrence
is sufficient for phenomenal experience.

152
00:10:28,210 --> 00:10:31,560
Wider recurrence involving
frontal areas adds access:

153
00:10:32,040 --> 00:10:33,920
report, memory, and control.

154
00:10:34,180 --> 00:10:38,890
The evidence includes the timing of
masking, which seems to work by interrupting

155
00:10:38,940 --> 00:10:43,990
recurrent processing, and experiments in
which a magnetic pulse over primary visual

156
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:49,270
cortex, delivered roughly a tenth of a
second after a stimulus, when feedback should

157
00:10:49,320 --> 00:10:51,820
be arriving, can abolish awareness of it.

158
00:10:52,040 --> 00:10:56,250
Recurrent processing theory is the
natural ally of Block's overflow view from

159
00:10:56,300 --> 00:11:01,100
episode seven: experience in local
loops, access for only some of it.

160
00:11:01,460 --> 00:11:04,200
Global workspace theory
sits on the other side:

161
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:10,300
no access, no experience. Its main
vulnerability is the refrigerator-light problem.

162
00:11:10,620 --> 00:11:15,770
If local recurrence produces experiences
that never reach report, how would we ever

163
00:11:15,820 --> 00:11:20,300
confirm they exist? For machines,
the theory has a clean implication:

164
00:11:20,700 --> 00:11:23,580
purely feedforward
computation isn't enough.

165
00:11:23,940 --> 00:11:30,270
Recurrence matters. Fourth, integrated
information theory, whose philosophy we met

166
00:11:30,320 --> 00:11:32,820
in episode nine. Here is the science.

167
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:38,390
I.I.T. predicts that the physical substrate
of experience lies mainly in a posterior

168
00:11:38,440 --> 00:11:41,960
hot zone of the cortex, rather
than in prefrontal cortex.

169
00:11:42,500 --> 00:11:47,190
The dreaming studies from last episode
fit that prediction, and the zap-and-zip

170
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:52,550
complexity measure was inspired by I.I.T.'s
idea that consciousness requires both

171
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:54,340
integration and differentiation.

172
00:11:55,040 --> 00:11:59,270
It also explains a striking fact about
the cerebellum, which contains about

173
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:04,450
sixty-nine billion of the brain's eighty-six
billion neurons, four out of five.

174
00:12:04,500 --> 00:12:09,890
Yet damage to it, even its complete absence
in rare people born without one, affects

175
00:12:09,940 --> 00:12:13,920
movement, speech and sometimes
learning, while consciousness remains.

176
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:19,390
On I.I.T.'s account, the cerebellum's
highly modular, parallel architecture

177
00:12:19,440 --> 00:12:24,300
generates little integrated information,
despite its huge neuron count.

178
00:12:25,040 --> 00:12:28,600
The skeptics of episode thirteen
lean on exactly this example.

179
00:12:29,260 --> 00:12:31,500
I.I.T. makes stranger predictions too.

180
00:12:31,820 --> 00:12:36,770
Because its measure, phi, depends on what
a system could do, not only on what it's

181
00:12:36,820 --> 00:12:41,150
doing, I.I.T. predicts that neurons
which are inactive but could fire still

182
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:45,990
contribute to experience, while the same
neurons silenced pharmacologically would

183
00:12:46,040 --> 00:12:50,900
not. That's testable in principle, though
nobody has yet done it convincingly.

184
00:12:51,100 --> 00:12:53,410
Now the criticisms, which are severe.

185
00:12:53,460 --> 00:12:58,650
The cost of computing phi exactly explodes
with the number of elements, so it has

186
00:12:58,700 --> 00:13:04,130
only been computed for tiny systems, using
software called PyPhi, developed by Will

187
00:13:04,180 --> 00:13:09,220
Mayner and colleagues. In practice,
every empirical test uses proxies.

188
00:13:09,660 --> 00:13:14,630
Then there's the unfolding argument, from
Adrien Doerig, Aaron Schurger, Michael

189
00:13:14,680 --> 00:13:16,960
Herzog, and colleagues in 2019.

190
00:13:17,300 --> 00:13:22,090
Any recurrent network can be unfolded
into a purely feedforward network that

191
00:13:22,140 --> 00:13:24,800
computes exactly the same
input-output function.

192
00:13:25,560 --> 00:13:30,570
I.I.T. assigns substantial phi to the
recurrent version and zero to the feedforward

193
00:13:30,620 --> 00:13:35,510
one. But every experiment we can run is
read through outputs, so it would treat the

194
00:13:35,560 --> 00:13:40,530
two identically. Either I.I.T.'s claims
about which system is conscious can never be

195
00:13:40,580 --> 00:13:45,910
tested, or they're false. And the same
argument, its authors point out, threatens

196
00:13:45,960 --> 00:13:47,380
recurrent processing theory.

197
00:13:47,740 --> 00:13:52,110
That's devastating if you think consciousness
must make a functional difference.

198
00:13:52,160 --> 00:13:58,010
Two behaviorally identical systems, and
the theory says one is conscious and the

199
00:13:58,060 --> 00:14:02,140
other isn't. Which is exactly
where I.I.T.'s defenders dig in.

200
00:14:02,820 --> 00:14:07,850
Consciousness, they argue, is about intrinsic
causal structure, which perturbation

201
00:14:07,900 --> 00:14:13,060
experiments like zap and zip can probe
directly, beyond input-output behavior.

202
00:14:13,260 --> 00:14:17,140
And they argue that the critics are
smuggling in a functionalist assumption.

203
00:14:17,460 --> 00:14:21,360
Whether that's a good reply depends
on your answer to episode seven.

204
00:14:21,410 --> 00:14:26,690
Then there's the logic-gate grid from
episode nine, which Tononi accepted rather

205
00:14:26,740 --> 00:14:31,070
than retreat. In September
2023, the tension boiled over.

206
00:14:31,120 --> 00:14:36,270
One hundred and twenty-four researchers
signed an open letter calling I.I.T.

207
00:14:36,320 --> 00:14:41,350
pseudoscience, arguing that its core claims
are untestable and that media coverage

208
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:42,920
had overstated its support.

209
00:14:43,180 --> 00:14:48,590
Many researchers, including some who reject
I.I.T., thought the label went much too

210
00:14:48,640 --> 00:14:52,560
far. In 2025, the fight moved
into Nature Neuroscience:

211
00:14:52,880 --> 00:14:58,310
an expanded critique; a reply from Tononi
and twenty-one co-authors arguing that the

212
00:14:58,360 --> 00:15:01,600
label reflected a bias toward
computational functionalism;

213
00:15:01,700 --> 00:15:07,050
and a third piece, by Alex Gomez-Marin and
Anil Seth, calling for the field to get

214
00:15:07,100 --> 00:15:11,280
past the name-calling. The
underlying question is a real one.

215
00:15:11,440 --> 00:15:16,790
Can a theory of consciousness be both
scientific and non-functionalist?

216
00:15:16,840 --> 00:15:21,630
Fifth, the predictive processing family,
which grows out of the predictive brain of

217
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:25,200
episode five. Anil Seth's
version is the beast machine.

218
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:29,810
If perception is the brain's best guess
about the causes of its sensory signals, a

219
00:15:29,860 --> 00:15:33,830
controlled hallucination, then the deepest
layer of prediction concerns the body

220
00:15:33,880 --> 00:15:38,790
itself: heartbeat, blood pressure,
gut, temperature, the signals called

221
00:15:38,840 --> 00:15:43,990
interoceptive. The brain predicts and
regulates these to keep the organism alive.

222
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:48,770
Seth argues that emotions, moods, and the
most basic feeling of being a self are

223
00:15:48,820 --> 00:15:50,440
rooted in that predictive regulation.

224
00:15:50,720 --> 00:15:55,380
We're conscious, on this view, not despite
being animals but because we are animals.

225
00:15:55,660 --> 00:15:59,180
There's suggestive evidence that
bodily signals shape perception.

226
00:15:59,580 --> 00:16:05,010
In 2014, Hyeong-Dong Park and colleagues
in Paris found that the brain's response to

227
00:16:05,060 --> 00:16:09,120
each heartbeat predicted whether people
would detect a faint visual stimulus.

228
00:16:09,820 --> 00:16:14,120
Lisa Feldman Barrett's theory of
constructed emotion makes a related claim:

229
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:17,950
emotions are predictions
grounded in interoception.

230
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:19,840
Mark Solms pushes further down.

231
00:16:20,060 --> 00:16:24,810
In The Hidden Spring, and in a 2019 paper
on the hard problem and the free energy

232
00:16:24,860 --> 00:16:29,150
principle, he argues that consciousness
originates not in the cortex but in the

233
00:16:29,200 --> 00:16:35,170
upper brainstem. It begins, he says, as
raw feeling, affect, which registers how

234
00:16:35,220 --> 00:16:37,710
well the organism is
doing at staying alive:

235
00:16:37,760 --> 00:16:42,460
rising uncertainty felt as unpleasant,
its resolution as pleasant.

236
00:16:42,680 --> 00:16:47,770
He draws on Jaak Panksepp's work on
emotional circuits deep in the brain, and on

237
00:16:47,820 --> 00:16:53,070
Björn Merker's observations of children
born with little or no cerebral cortex, who

238
00:16:53,120 --> 00:16:57,910
nonetheless seem alert, emotionally
responsive, and able to recognize the people

239
00:16:57,960 --> 00:17:02,470
they know. Merker's target article
in Behavioral and Brain Sciences drew

240
00:17:02,520 --> 00:17:05,100
commentaries showing
how contested this is:

241
00:17:05,200 --> 00:17:09,950
some of these children retain islands of
cortex, and critics argue the behaviors

242
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,320
fall short of establishing experience.

243
00:17:12,940 --> 00:17:18,170
Antonio Damasio, from Descartes' Error
onward, has long argued that feeling is

244
00:17:18,220 --> 00:17:20,180
grounded in the body's homeostasis.

245
00:17:20,480 --> 00:17:25,770
On the other side, Joseph LeDoux and Richard
Brown argue that emotional experiences

246
00:17:25,820 --> 00:17:30,250
are higher-order cortical states, and
that the deep survival circuits produce

247
00:17:30,300 --> 00:17:32,800
defensive responses, not felt fear.

248
00:17:33,120 --> 00:17:38,230
Which side is right decides what we should
say about fish, newborns, and patients

249
00:17:38,280 --> 00:17:44,450
with little cortex. And in 2025, Ruben
Laukkonen, Karl Friston, and Shamil Chandaria

250
00:17:44,500 --> 00:17:47,920
proposed an active inference
theory called A Beautiful Loop.

251
00:17:48,100 --> 00:17:49,820
It names three conditions.

252
00:17:50,120 --> 00:17:54,960
A unified, simulated model of the world,
which they call the epistemic field.

253
00:17:55,120 --> 00:17:59,830
A competition among inferences, in
which those that best reduce long-term

254
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:02,090
uncertainty win and are bound together.

255
00:18:02,140 --> 00:18:07,190
And epistemic depth, in which the model's
beliefs are shared recurrently through its

256
00:18:07,240 --> 00:18:11,400
own hierarchy, so that the model
contains knowledge of its own existence.

257
00:18:11,820 --> 00:18:16,830
That's the loop. The authors use the
meditative states from last episode as test

258
00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:19,720
cases. What does this
family imply for machines?

259
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:21,860
It depends which member you ask.

260
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:25,880
On Seth's beast-machine view,
prediction alone isn't enough;

261
00:18:26,240 --> 00:18:30,770
prediction in the service of staying alive
is what matters, so today's disembodied

262
00:18:30,820 --> 00:18:32,320
systems are poor candidates.

263
00:18:32,820 --> 00:18:37,830
On a more purely computational reading of
active inference, an artificial agent with

264
00:18:37,880 --> 00:18:40,740
the right self-modeling
architecture could qualify.

265
00:18:41,960 --> 00:18:46,950
Sixth, Michael Graziano's attention schema
theory, the illusionist's mechanism from

266
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:50,980
episode eight. As a scientific theory
it makes a specific prediction:

267
00:18:51,360 --> 00:18:53,800
awareness should matter
for controlling attention.

268
00:18:54,060 --> 00:18:59,030
And that's roughly what experiments by
Taylor Webb, Graziano, and colleagues found

269
00:18:59,080 --> 00:19:03,340
in 2016. Cues people weren't aware
of still captured their attention.

270
00:19:03,860 --> 00:19:08,210
But without awareness, people controlled
their attention less well, holding it less

271
00:19:08,260 --> 00:19:11,900
stably. Transformers are
literally built out of attention.

272
00:19:12,280 --> 00:19:14,160
Do they have attention schemas?

273
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:19,980
Not in Graziano's sense. Attention in
a transformer is a routing mechanism:

274
00:19:20,440 --> 00:19:23,060
each position weighs
information from the others.

275
00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:27,040
It doesn't come with a model of
that attention used to control it.

276
00:19:27,440 --> 00:19:31,790
Whether something like that could be
learned implicitly is an open question, and

277
00:19:31,840 --> 00:19:35,920
it's one of the indicators researchers
check for, as we'll see next episode.

278
00:19:37,140 --> 00:19:43,110
Seventh, unlimited associative learning,
from Simona Ginsburg and Eva Jablonka.

279
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:46,820
Rather than a mechanism, it
identifies a transition marker:

280
00:19:47,060 --> 00:19:51,410
an evolved capacity whose presence
indicates that consciousness has arisen.

281
00:19:51,460 --> 00:19:56,750
Unlimited associative learning is open-ended
learning about novel, compound stimuli

282
00:19:56,800 --> 00:20:01,620
and actions: combinations of
features, across time gaps, in chains.

283
00:20:02,220 --> 00:20:06,690
On their account, it first evolved around
the Cambrian explosion, more than five

284
00:20:06,740 --> 00:20:10,800
hundred million years ago, and
consciousness evolved at least three times:

285
00:20:11,040 --> 00:20:14,200
in vertebrates, in arthropods,
and in cephalopods.

286
00:20:14,580 --> 00:20:18,110
Language models learn
associations open-endedly.

287
00:20:18,160 --> 00:20:19,870
By that marker, they're in.

288
00:20:19,920 --> 00:20:22,720
Ginsburg and Jablonka would say no.

289
00:20:22,980 --> 00:20:28,650
In a 2021 paper with Zohar Bronfman on
when robots might be sentient, they argued

290
00:20:28,700 --> 00:20:32,590
that a machine would need this kind
of learning tied to a body, and to one

291
00:20:32,640 --> 00:20:35,060
overriding value, keeping itself going.

292
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:37,240
Language models have neither.

293
00:20:37,420 --> 00:20:41,630
And that shows the general problem with
transplanting any marker calibrated on

294
00:20:41,680 --> 00:20:45,440
animals. A few other
theories deserve a mention.

295
00:20:45,960 --> 00:20:51,590
Dendritic integration theory, from Jaan
Aru, Mototaka Suzuki, and Matthew Larkum,

296
00:20:51,640 --> 00:20:56,430
ties consciousness to a cellular mechanism
in large cortical neurons, and in

297
00:20:56,480 --> 00:20:59,790
rodents, anesthetics appear to
work in part by disrupting it.

298
00:20:59,840 --> 00:21:05,490
Cyriel Pennartz's neurorepresentationalism
treats consciousness as the brain's

299
00:21:05,540 --> 00:21:08,160
multimodal, situational
model of the world.

300
00:21:08,340 --> 00:21:13,610
And electromagnetic field theories, like
Johnjoe McFadden's, locate the integration

301
00:21:13,660 --> 00:21:16,520
of experience in the
brain's own electric field.

302
00:21:16,600 --> 00:21:22,350
The skeptics of episode thirteen cite recent
work from Earl Miller's lab suggesting

303
00:21:22,400 --> 00:21:27,750
that the brain's electric fields help
coordinate activity across the cortex, as one

304
00:21:27,800 --> 00:21:31,540
way biology might matter. Now the trials.

305
00:21:31,800 --> 00:21:37,170
The first, and so far the most important,
was the Cogitate Consortium's test of

306
00:21:37,220 --> 00:21:41,390
global neuronal workspace theory
against integrated information theory.

307
00:21:41,440 --> 00:21:45,470
Two hundred and fifty-six participants
were tested with three methods:

308
00:21:45,520 --> 00:21:52,110
fMRI, M.E.G., and electrodes implanted
in the brains of thirty-four epilepsy

309
00:21:52,160 --> 00:21:57,810
patients. They saw faces, objects, letters,
and meaningless symbols, at different

310
00:21:57,860 --> 00:22:02,920
orientations and durations, sometimes
relevant to their task and sometimes not.

311
00:22:03,340 --> 00:22:05,360
The theories made opposing predictions.

312
00:22:06,160 --> 00:22:10,070
I.I.T. predicted that conscious content
would be maintained in the back of the brain

313
00:22:10,120 --> 00:22:14,570
for as long as the stimulus was seen,
with sustained synchronization between

314
00:22:14,620 --> 00:22:20,110
posterior regions. Global workspace theory
predicted ignition in prefrontal cortex

315
00:22:20,160 --> 00:22:25,050
when the stimulus appeared and again when
it disappeared, and that prefrontal cortex

316
00:22:25,100 --> 00:22:27,080
would carry the details of what was seen.

317
00:22:27,240 --> 00:22:31,980
The results, published in Nature
in April 2025, challenged both.

318
00:22:32,300 --> 00:22:37,360
Some predictions held up. Some sites at the
back tracked how long a stimulus lasted,

319
00:22:37,640 --> 00:22:40,180
and prefrontal cortex
responded when it appeared.

320
00:22:40,580 --> 00:22:42,060
But key predictions failed.

321
00:22:42,280 --> 00:22:46,980
There was no sustained, content-specific
synchronization within posterior cortex,

322
00:22:47,400 --> 00:22:50,520
which the authors called the
most direct challenge to I.I.T.

323
00:22:50,800 --> 00:22:53,520
And there was no ignition
when the stimulus disappeared.

324
00:22:54,060 --> 00:22:58,730
Prefrontal cortex carried information
about the category of what was seen, face

325
00:22:58,780 --> 00:23:03,830
versus object, but not about its exact
identity, and little or nothing about its

326
00:23:03,880 --> 00:23:08,180
orientation. That's a serious
problem for global workspace theory.

327
00:23:08,660 --> 00:23:11,580
So the first decisive test was indecisive.

328
00:23:12,280 --> 00:23:17,070
Decisive about some predictions, indecisive
about the theories, which is how science

329
00:23:17,120 --> 00:23:21,910
usually works. Both camps disputed
parts of the interpretation, and the

330
00:23:21,960 --> 00:23:25,740
pseudoscience letter erupted a few months
after the first results were presented.

331
00:23:26,100 --> 00:23:28,400
But the method is the real result.

332
00:23:28,780 --> 00:23:33,270
Theories made risky, preregistered
predictions, and some of those predictions

333
00:23:33,320 --> 00:23:36,330
failed. More rounds are under way.

334
00:23:36,380 --> 00:23:38,860
Cogitate's second experiment
uses a video game.

335
00:23:39,360 --> 00:23:43,850
People play while faces and objects appear
in the background, so their attention is

336
00:23:43,900 --> 00:23:47,990
pulled away from them, and later watch
replays of gameplay while attending to those

337
00:23:48,040 --> 00:23:53,980
images. It was introduced in posters at
the field's 2025 meeting in Crete, with one

338
00:23:54,030 --> 00:23:58,770
hundred and thirty-nine
participants across fMRI and M.E.G.

339
00:23:58,820 --> 00:24:02,600
Those posters showed that the game
works, and gave early, mixed results:

340
00:24:02,940 --> 00:24:07,130
weak support for a key workspace prediction,
and only partial support for one of

341
00:24:07,180 --> 00:24:11,970
I.I.T.'s. None of it is peer reviewed yet,
and no verdict on the theories has been

342
00:24:12,020 --> 00:24:16,810
published. A separate collaboration,
called Intrepid, is testing I.I.T.

343
00:24:16,860 --> 00:24:21,670
against active inference and against
neurorepresentationalism, with four experiments

344
00:24:21,720 --> 00:24:23,760
and independent replications planned.

345
00:24:24,200 --> 00:24:25,780
Its results are pending too.

346
00:24:26,780 --> 00:24:29,860
What would settle things?
A few candidate tests.

347
00:24:30,280 --> 00:24:33,580
I.I.T.'s inactive-neuron prediction,
if anyone can make it work.

348
00:24:33,960 --> 00:24:38,320
Lesion and stimulation studies of
prefrontal cortex that are large enough, and

349
00:24:38,370 --> 00:24:40,790
preregistered, to settle
the front-versus-back fight.

350
00:24:40,840 --> 00:24:44,720
No-report designs that cleanly
separate experience from access.

351
00:24:45,020 --> 00:24:50,370
And the extreme states of last episode,
cessations, ketamine dreams, awareness

352
00:24:50,420 --> 00:24:53,540
without content, which any
complete theory has to handle.

353
00:24:53,980 --> 00:24:58,310
Meanwhile, let me line the theories up
against the question this series is building

354
00:24:58,360 --> 00:25:03,510
toward. Global workspace theory says a
machine could be conscious if it has a

355
00:25:03,560 --> 00:25:07,910
limited-capacity workspace whose contents
are broadcast to many specialized

356
00:25:07,960 --> 00:25:13,070
processes. Higher-order theories say
yes, if the machine monitors its own

357
00:25:13,120 --> 00:25:14,840
representations in the right way.

358
00:25:15,080 --> 00:25:18,640
Recurrent processing theory
requires genuine recurrence.

359
00:25:19,360 --> 00:25:24,170
Attention schema theory requires a model
of the machine's own attention, used to

360
00:25:24,220 --> 00:25:29,580
control it. Integrated information theory
says no for conventional digital hardware,

361
00:25:29,820 --> 00:25:34,450
whatever the software. The beast-machine
version of predictive processing makes

362
00:25:34,500 --> 00:25:38,720
systems that aren't regulating a
living body very unlikely candidates.

363
00:25:39,420 --> 00:25:43,810
And unlimited associative learning,
on its authors' account, would require

364
00:25:43,860 --> 00:25:48,830
open-ended learning in a body, driven by
something like self-preservation, which

365
00:25:48,880 --> 00:25:53,690
today's systems lack. So whether today's
AI systems are candidates for consciousness

366
00:25:53,740 --> 00:25:58,200
depends almost entirely on which theory
you believe, and no theory has won.

367
00:25:58,380 --> 00:26:00,380
That isn't a reason for despair.

368
00:26:00,580 --> 00:26:04,500
It's the starting point for the most
careful attempts yet to assess machines.

369
00:26:05,090 --> 00:26:10,330
In 2023, a group of nineteen researchers
turned most of these theories, though not

370
00:26:10,380 --> 00:26:15,030
I.I.T., into a checklist of architectural
indicators that you can look for in an AI

371
00:26:15,080 --> 00:26:17,520
system. That's where Part Three begins.
